-- title:
--   Hecke–Galois datum's residual representation is equivalent to ρ̄_{W,p}
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $p$ (with $p \neq 2$) and a Weierstrass model $W$ over $\mathbb{Z}$ with $\Delta_W \neq 0$ which is semistable in the project's sense (no prime dividing $\Delta_W$ divides $c_4$). Assume the $p$-torsion of the points of $W_{\mathbb{Q}}$ over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ has cardinality $p^2$ (`hcard₁`) and that the Galois action on that $p$-torsion by $\mathbb{Z}/p$-endomorphisms is trivial on the automorphisms fixing some finite subextension $L/\mathbb{Q}$ of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ (`hker`); these two data produce the mod $p$ representation `residualGaloisRepOf`, the two-dimensional $\mathbb{Z}/p$-representation on the $p$-torsion. Let $S$ be a finite set of naturals, all prime, containing $p$ and every prime dividing $\Delta_W$. Let $\mathcal{O}$ be a complete discrete valuation ring of characteristic $0$ with finite residue field and $p$ in its maximal ideal, and $\iota : \mathbb{Z}/p \to k_{\mathcal{O}}$ a ring homomorphism such that the base change of the above representation along $\iota$ is absolutely irreducible (irreducible after base change to an algebraic closure of $k_{\mathcal{O}}$). Let $N \neq 0$ be a level whose prime divisors lie in $S$, with $q^2 \mid N$ for each $q \in S$, $q \neq p$, with $p \mid N$ and $p^2 \nmid N$ whenever $p$ divides $\Delta_W$ or $p \nmid a_p(W)$, and $p \nmid N$ when $p \nmid \Delta_W$ and $p \mid a_p(W)$. Let $\theta$ be a ring homomorphism from the Hecke algebra [`CuspForm.heckeAlgebra N 2 S`](def/CuspForm_HeckeAlgebra.html#L18) (the $\mathbb{Z}$-algebra generated by the $T_\ell$ for $\ell \nmid N$, $\ell \notin S$, and the $U_q$ for $q \mid N$, $q \notin S$, acting on weight-two cusp forms on $\Gamma_0(N)$) to $k_{\mathcal{O}}$ with $\theta(T_\ell)$ the residue of $a_\ell(W)$ for all primes $\ell \nmid N$ outside $S$. Let $T$ be a complete local Noetherian $\mathcal{O}$-algebra, finite and free as an $\mathcal{O}$-module, with local structure map, and let $H$ be a [`CuspForm.HeckeGaloisRepDatum N S 𝒪 θ T`](def/CuspForm_HeckeGaloisRepDatum.html#L8): a ring homomorphism $\pi$ from the Hecke algebra to $T$ whose residues recover $\theta$ through $k_{\mathcal{O}} \to k_T$, with $T$ generated over $\mathcal{O}$ by the image of $\pi$, with every lift $\chi : \mathbb{T} \to \mathcal{O}$ of $\theta$ factoring as $\psi \circ \pi$ for some $\mathcal{O}$-algebra map $\psi : T \to \mathcal{O}$, with $k_T$ generated by the image of $\mathcal{O}$ (surjectivity of the residue of $\mathcal{O} \to T$), together with a rank-two adically continuous Galois representation $\rho$ over $T$ whose Frobenius characteristic polynomials at primes $\ell \nmid N$ outside $S$ are $X^2 - \pi(T_\ell)X + \ell$ and whose residual representation is absolutely irreducible. The conclusion is that the residual representation of $H.\rho$ (base change of $\rho$ to $k_T$) admits a Galois-equivariant $k_T$-linear isomorphism to the mod $p$ representation of $W$ base changed first along $\iota$ and then along the induced map $k_{\mathcal{O}} \to k_T$. (The hypotheses $p \neq 2$, $\Delta_W \neq 0$, semistability, primality of the members of $S$, $p \in \mathfrak{m}_{\mathcal{O}}$, and all the conditions on $N$ at $p$ and at the primes of $S$ are carried but not used; what is used is that $S$ contains $p$ and the bad primes, that prime divisors of $N$ lie in $S$, the trace identity for $\theta$, and the two absolute irreducibility hypotheses.)
--
--   This is the residual comparison step in modularity lifting for semistable elliptic curves: the mod $p$ representation attached to a Hecke eigensystem whose $T_\ell$-values match the Frobenius traces $a_\ell(W)$ is the mod $p$ representation of the curve, up to the coefficient base changes recorded in the statement. Classically this is Chebotarev's density theorem combined with the Brauer–Nesbitt argument for two absolutely irreducible two-dimensional representations with matching characteristic polynomials; the formal version asserts only the existence of an equivariant isomorphism (`IsEquiv`), and compares the Hecke-side residual representation with the curve's representation after base change along $\iota$ and then along the residue-field map of $\mathcal{O} \to T$, rather than asserting any literal equality. It is used in the verification that a Hecke–Galois datum's residual representation satisfies the ordinarity condition at $p$, and, further downstream, in the modularity lifting statements at conductor level for $p = 3$ and $p = 5$ which feed the main line of the argument.
